WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
We've released a lot of new great stuff in Connect

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.500
over the last couple of months,

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.580
but the one thing that I want to demo today

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:11.980
is my new favorite way to do publishing in Connect.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.600
It's called Trusted Publishing,

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.240
and it helps you go from manual deployment

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.180
of a content asset

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.120
to keyless GitHub Action-style pipeline deployment.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.940
And by keyless here, we mean that you're no longer required

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.939
to use a Connect API key to drive your deployments.

00:00:33.639 --> 00:00:37.419
So as before, if you've set up a CICD pipeline

00:00:37.419 --> 00:00:39.720
using something like GitHub Actions,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.619
you're more than likely using either your own API key

00:00:43.619 --> 00:00:46.840
or one from a user you've created

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:50.119
specifically to act as a service account-style user

00:00:50.119 --> 00:00:51.360
in Connect.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.639
And not only is this a better security posture,

00:00:55.639 --> 00:00:59.459
allowing the pipeline to do a token exchange

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.180
with Connect itself,

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.820
it also removes that necessity

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.860
so that other actors,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.360
be them other people I'm collaborating with

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.860
on this repository or bots, whoever,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.940
they're no longer using my API key.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.980
And so the audit logs and who's doing what

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.860
and tracing things back

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.339
to use of that pipeline

00:01:23.339 --> 00:01:26.419
instead of that key becomes a lot clearer.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:29.400
So setting this up is pretty simple.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.980
We've tried to make this as simple as possible.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.980
It's available and enabled by default in the July release,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.820
but it's only available for enhanced

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.019
and advanced Connect licenses right now.

00:01:43.019 --> 00:01:46.300
So you'll deploy your content once,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.180
you set up your GitHub repository,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.739
you set up trusted publishing on the Connect side,

00:01:52.739 --> 00:01:56.379
you set up your scaffold for GitHub actions

00:01:56.379 --> 00:01:58.900
or whatever pipeline you're building.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.739
We have a new Connect action skill

00:02:01.739 --> 00:02:04.139
that makes this really easy.

00:02:04.139 --> 00:02:06.500
You don't forget to pin your dependencies,

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.259
even though you've used something

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:09.960
maybe like push-button deployment,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.339
you still need to have a manifest or requirements file

00:02:13.339 --> 00:02:15.899
in the repository for this to function,

00:02:15.899 --> 00:02:17.820
and then you run it.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.419
So I'll show you what this looks like.

00:02:19.419 --> 00:02:23.820
I have a piece of content that I've been working on lately,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.500
sort of wanted to explore

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.940
the Imagination Library impact in numbers.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.179
With Dolly Parton's recent passing,

00:02:34.179 --> 00:02:36.539
I started looking into just how amazing

00:02:36.539 --> 00:02:40.139
this organization is and the sheer numbers

00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:44.020
and scale and impact of what they've accomplished

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.220
over the past 31 years is amazing.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.139
So I was having a time building this out

00:02:52.139 --> 00:02:55.500
as a Shiny application and push-button published it

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.020
using the Posit publisher from Positron.

00:03:01.259 --> 00:03:03.220
But I was thinking this morning

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.220
that like these donate buttons,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.380
I'd love for them to pop more.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.580
So I was gonna transition this

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.779
to using the new trusted publishing model.

00:03:14.779 --> 00:03:17.619
So open this up, I go to the source pane

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:21.740
and I see I have a new trusted publishing area here.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:25.619
And you add your trusted publisher through this form.

00:03:25.619 --> 00:03:27.059
This is a little bit tricky.

00:03:27.059 --> 00:03:28.259
If it's a new repository,

00:03:28.259 --> 00:03:32.580
you do need to use the new immutable IDs format

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.539
for setting up your repo.

00:03:34.539 --> 00:03:36.940
But we have that documented in the user guide,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.339
very easy to find, run it.

00:03:40.539 --> 00:03:42.339
Once that's set up,

00:03:42.339 --> 00:03:45.500
then I can go back to Positron

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.139
and set up the rest of the OWL here.

00:03:48.139 --> 00:03:49.539
So what I've done behind the scenes,

00:03:49.539 --> 00:03:52.339
and don't worry about how small this is,

00:03:52.339 --> 00:03:55.619
I've set up a Git init.

00:03:55.619 --> 00:03:58.179
I created my repository in GitHub.

00:03:58.179 --> 00:04:01.020
I used the new Connect Action skill

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.380
to scaffold my workflow for GitHub Actions.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.979
I've made the changes using Posit Assistant

00:04:07.979 --> 00:04:10.779
to update my donate buttons here.

00:04:10.779 --> 00:04:13.339
So now they look really nice.

00:04:13.339 --> 00:04:16.299
And I've been working in a branch

00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:21.299
because I have good Git practices here.

00:04:22.339 --> 00:04:27.220
And I'm gonna go ahead and push this up to my repository.

00:04:28.339 --> 00:04:30.619
If I go back up to my repository,

00:04:30.619 --> 00:04:35.339
add a new branch, style the donate buttons is good.

00:04:35.339 --> 00:04:39.019
I'm gonna create a pull request here.

00:04:39.019 --> 00:04:42.140
And as soon as that starts running,

00:04:42.179 --> 00:04:46.660
it's going to activate my deployment pipeline.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.299
And because this is going into a branch

00:04:50.299 --> 00:04:52.579
and not directly to main,

00:04:52.579 --> 00:04:56.299
it's going to do something else that's really cool.

00:04:56.299 --> 00:05:00.820
It's going to deploy to Connect, but as a draft,

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:05.820
rather than as a bundle that gets immediately activated.

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:10.220
So once this runs, I'll take a couple seconds here

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:14.299
to go through all of the process of bundling this up

00:05:14.299 --> 00:05:17.140
and sending it up to my Connect server.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:20.579
I'll be able to go directly from here

00:05:20.579 --> 00:05:24.540
to that draft version of my new content item

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.540
with the styled donate buttons running on Connect.

00:05:29.579 --> 00:05:30.940
So here it is.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.859
Here's the link to it.

00:05:32.859 --> 00:05:34.579
Gonna go straight there.

00:05:34.579 --> 00:05:36.859
It'll set it up.

00:05:36.859 --> 00:05:38.540
I have my new buttons here.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.339
They look great.

00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:43.140
And you'll see here that there's a banner

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.940
and it says back to active version

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.579
because it's indicating that this is a draft.

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:50.739
This isn't the active version of my application

00:05:50.739 --> 00:05:52.420
that viewers are seeing

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:56.059
if they come to this piece of content on Connect right now.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:01.500
And so if I wanted to accept this version

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:06.500
as the one that I want to promote to active,

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:10.579
I do that also in the source pane here.

00:06:10.579 --> 00:06:12.660
I can go to view all my bundles

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.019
and I see here I've been doing quite a bit of publishing

00:06:17.019 --> 00:06:21.019
over the last couple of days from my trusted publisher

00:06:21.019 --> 00:06:23.179
and they're all labeled as bot.

00:06:23.179 --> 00:06:25.739
I have to go down here and see that

00:06:25.739 --> 00:06:28.179
this was the first one created by me

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:32.779
and the rest of them are created by the pipeline.

00:06:32.779 --> 00:06:35.339
But I can still open up this metadata here.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.260
And see that this is the one that came from branch two

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:46.260
and the source author is here, it's me.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.100
If the source author was somebody else,

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.339
it'd be somebody else there.

00:06:49.339 --> 00:06:53.660
And so I have visibility into who is driving the action

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.459
coming from that pipeline.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:00.619
And now if I want to activate my current draft,

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:02.940
hit the activate button

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.260
and it's going to activate that bundle

00:07:07.019 --> 00:07:09.019
and replace what we were seeing

00:07:09.019 --> 00:07:12.100
and what all the viewers were seeing with the new one,

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.660
with my styled buttons.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:22.940
So I've really been enjoying this new flow.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.700
I think it's pretty great.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:28.619
And at any time you can go back

00:07:28.619 --> 00:07:30.899
and use the bundles modal

00:07:30.899 --> 00:07:35.899
to explore previous iterations of bundles

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:40.299
that and look at them in draft form.

00:07:40.299 --> 00:07:43.619
So I can use this to check out a draft.

00:07:44.619 --> 00:07:47.420
We'll spin it up again and run the content.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:52.619
And if I want to share this with somebody

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:57.899
and have it be viewed like nicely,

00:07:57.899 --> 00:08:00.220
I can even open it up in standalone mode

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.220
and they'll get this warning banner up in the top.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.059
This is not an unpublished draft.

00:08:05.059 --> 00:08:07.700
Published version is here.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.540
So that way, if you're collaborating with somebody,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:14.019
if you're sharing around these draft versions,

00:08:14.019 --> 00:08:17.459
they don't get confused about what's actually live

00:08:17.459 --> 00:08:20.700
versus what is just being iterated on.

00:08:23.059 --> 00:08:26.820
If you, for whatever reason, don't want this banner,

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:30.019
we have a way to turn that off as well.

00:08:30.019 --> 00:08:31.779
So just toggle it off

00:08:31.779 --> 00:08:34.500
and then your draft banner will be removed.

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:40.500
One of the other really nice things I want to point out

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:44.739
in this newer version of Connect here,

00:08:44.739 --> 00:08:49.020
and this is released, I believe, in July as well,

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.979
is the bundle viewer.

00:08:51.979 --> 00:08:53.619
So now, as before,

00:08:53.619 --> 00:08:56.659
when you needed to inspect the contents

00:08:56.659 --> 00:08:59.020
of any of your bundles,

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:00.500
you would need to download them

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:02.700
and then open them up on your local machine

00:09:02.739 --> 00:09:05.539
or in your workbench, wherever.

00:09:05.539 --> 00:09:09.099
But now you can actually have a read-only view

00:09:09.099 --> 00:09:14.099
into each of your readable files directly in the bundle

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:18.500
if, for whatever reason, you need that.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:21.020
And there's a lot of times when it is really nice

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.179
to have that quick read-only reference here on Connect

00:09:24.179 --> 00:09:26.619
without having to download things.

00:09:26.619 --> 00:09:27.940
You can't edit here.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:30.820
It is read-only, but at least you can reference

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.780
and compare between bundles.

00:09:36.419 --> 00:09:38.979
All right, let's go back to the active version

00:09:38.979 --> 00:09:41.619
and I'll show you one more cool thing.

00:09:41.619 --> 00:09:45.820
You don't need this button all the time,

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.460
but when you do need it, it's quite handy to have.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:49.619
You might notice that, like,

00:09:49.619 --> 00:09:52.260
for your static or rendered content,

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.820
you have a refresh button that you can use

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:57.659
to re-render the content item itself.

00:09:57.659 --> 00:09:59.820
You didn't have that for interactive content

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:03.380
like this app is, because most of the time,

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.380
whenever you start a new session,

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:08.140
you get refreshed data, it's interactive,

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:10.539
you don't need to rebuild it all the time.

00:10:10.539 --> 00:10:13.539
But when you do need to rebuild content

00:10:13.539 --> 00:10:16.700
after, like, installing a missing system dependency

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.460
or updating your server config,

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.059
lots of admin-type functionality

00:10:23.059 --> 00:10:26.460
often require a content rebuild.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.900
Then having this available

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.219
and not having to go through

00:10:31.219 --> 00:10:33.700
other, like, more backhanded mechanisms

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:36.780
to accomplish a full content rebuild is really nice.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:39.219
So I'm glad that we've added that here in the UI.

00:10:41.739 --> 00:10:46.659
Let's see, those are the current things.

00:10:46.659 --> 00:10:49.580
I do have a quick preview of things

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:51.619
that will be coming out next week as well

00:10:51.619 --> 00:10:53.299
in the September release.

00:10:53.299 --> 00:10:55.659
I wanted to call out specifically

00:10:55.659 --> 00:10:57.979
our new Notification Center,

00:10:57.979 --> 00:11:00.900
and then also a new functionality

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.979
that allows you to manage publisher permissions

00:11:04.979 --> 00:11:06.580
in the UI itself.

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:09.340
So I'll show both of those really quickly here.

00:11:11.619 --> 00:11:14.979
Our new Notification Center

00:11:14.979 --> 00:11:18.539
is this bell icon next to your username here.

00:11:18.539 --> 00:11:21.140
And you'll see all sorts of new types

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:25.619
of notifications appear here as you upgrade Connect.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:28.140
One of the first sets we've started with

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:30.299
is the What's New feed.

00:11:30.299 --> 00:11:35.299
That takes you to the feed of things that are new,

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:37.859
either features or announcements,

00:11:37.859 --> 00:11:40.780
like an invitation to this webinar,

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:44.140
and also permissions requests.

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:49.140
So you can manage requests for access to content

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.500
straight from your bell,

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.299
and you get notified when you have new ones

00:11:54.299 --> 00:11:56.099
instead of just in your email.

00:11:56.099 --> 00:11:58.219
You click through and you see this one

00:11:58.219 --> 00:12:00.659
is one that got resolved yesterday,

00:12:00.659 --> 00:12:04.299
so I don't need to take action on that.

00:12:04.299 --> 00:12:08.059
But if I were, I could click through

00:12:08.059 --> 00:12:12.340
and then immediately take action on the content item.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:13.940
It takes you straight to it.

00:12:15.619 --> 00:12:17.619
The other thing that I want to point out

00:12:17.619 --> 00:12:19.780
that's coming in September

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:24.780
is our new UI-based role permission editor.

00:12:25.419 --> 00:12:28.900
This too is going to get more powerful over time.

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:31.020
But in September, what we'll have

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:34.539
is the ability to edit publisher permissions

00:12:34.539 --> 00:12:36.419
that you historically would have had

00:12:36.419 --> 00:12:39.700
to have set in the Connect config file.

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:41.900
So here gives you like a quick toggle,

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:44.500
and this is global for all publishers

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:46.700
on your Connect server.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:48.380
You can give them permissions

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:52.260
to do things like update the run as settings,

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:57.260
create vanity URLs, remove vanity URLs, and upload bundles.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:12:59.380
Hopefully this makes it a lot easier

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:02.900
to manage the permission sets that go along with each role.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:06.059
And our intention is for this to become

00:13:06.059 --> 00:13:08.260
a lot more powerful to build out

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:12.179
into eventually custom roles and permissions.

00:13:12.179 --> 00:13:14.419
So I'm very excited about the direction of that.

00:13:14.419 --> 00:13:17.419
The September release won't have full customization,

00:13:17.419 --> 00:13:18.700
but we're starting here.

